WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.612
Welcome to Building the
Daily Checks Workbook.

2
00:00:02.612 --> 00:00:06.812
This is Chapter 1: the daily
checks, and where each one lives.

3
00:00:06.812 --> 00:00:09.308
Before anything is drawn on a dashboard,

4
00:00:09.308 --> 00:00:13.468
each daily check in the operator
runbook gets one row: what it reads,

5
00:00:13.468 --> 00:00:17.099
where else to look, and whether
a workbook can show it at all.

6
00:00:17.099 --> 00:00:21.415
We'll start from what Microsoft Learn
gives you, see what it leaves to you,

7
00:00:21.415 --> 00:00:25.438
read the kit's manifest and its
twelve tabs, the domain controllers

8
00:00:25.438 --> 00:00:29.387
and the request-driven admin changes
among them, and walk two labs.

9
00:00:29.387 --> 00:00:32.159
The chapter has been
reviewed and approved,

10
00:00:32.159 --> 00:00:35.990
and the code is illustrative,
tested against fixtures.

11
00:00:39.740 --> 00:00:41.052
Why start with a list?

12
00:00:41.052 --> 00:00:44.569
The operator runbook asks for
twelve checks every morning,

13
00:00:44.569 --> 00:00:47.153
and most of them are done
one portal at a time.

14
00:00:47.153 --> 00:00:51.928
Some of that data is already in the Sentinel
workspace; some of it never will be.

15
00:00:51.928 --> 00:00:55.621
So the goal is to say, for every
daily check, which table shows it,

16
00:00:55.621 --> 00:00:59.169
where the operator looks when
Log Analytics doesn't have it,

17
00:00:59.169 --> 00:01:02.065
and how much a workbook
tab can be trusted to show.

18
00:01:02.065 --> 00:01:06.173
It's for the developers who maintain
the kit and the security operations

19
00:01:06.173 --> 00:01:10.160
lead who owns the workspace, and
you don't need a tenant until Lab 2.

20
00:01:13.910 --> 00:01:15.935
Here's what Microsoft Learn gives you.

21
00:01:15.935 --> 00:01:19.320
Its guidance on visualizations
recommends workbooks for

22
00:01:19.320 --> 00:01:22.635
reports across Azure sources,
and lists the other options.

23
00:01:22.635 --> 00:01:26.968
Its table reference pages give
each source table and its columns,

24
00:01:26.968 --> 00:01:30.098
from sign-in logs to
Intune's compliance export.

25
00:01:30.098 --> 00:01:33.823
Sentinel's health table covers
only the connectors Learn lists,

26
00:01:33.823 --> 00:01:36.423
and only once health
monitoring is turned on.

27
00:01:36.423 --> 00:01:39.607
The Logs Ingestion API
sends your own records

28
00:01:39.607 --> 00:01:42.457
to a custom table ending
in underscore C L.

29
00:01:42.457 --> 00:01:47.234
Entra Health monitoring is a preview
in the admin center and by e-mail.

30
00:01:47.234 --> 00:01:51.710
Intune exports compliance once a
day, up to forty-eight hours late.

31
00:01:51.710 --> 00:01:54.099
And for the servers that stay on premises,

32
00:01:54.099 --> 00:01:59.090
the Azure Monitor Agent sends their event
logs and a heartbeat to the workspace.

33
00:02:02.840 --> 00:02:04.065
What does it leave to you?

34
00:02:04.065 --> 00:02:08.353
Learn gives a table per source, and
the runbook gives the checks in prose,

35
00:02:08.353 --> 00:02:11.115
but nobody says which
table shows which check.

36
00:02:11.115 --> 00:02:14.202
So the kit has one manifest
row per daily check.

37
00:02:14.202 --> 00:02:19.281
Some checks have no data in Log Analytics
at all, and a tab built on missing data

38
00:02:19.281 --> 00:02:21.979
looks healthy: those become link-out tabs,

39
00:02:21.979 --> 00:02:24.677
with no tables and the click path instead.

40
00:02:24.677 --> 00:02:29.177
Freshness is per source, so each
expected table gets its own limit.

41
00:02:29.177 --> 00:02:35.077
And because availability is spread across
pages, each tab carries its GCC note.

42
00:02:35.077 --> 00:02:37.770
Every row here is pinned by a test.

43
00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:42.857
Here's the manifest.

44
00:02:42.857 --> 00:02:45.419
Twelve tabs, one per daily check.

45
00:02:45.419 --> 00:02:48.587
Ten read tables, among them
the synchronization probe,

46
00:02:48.587 --> 00:02:53.091
which now also reads the synchronization
servers' own events and heartbeats,

47
00:02:53.091 --> 00:02:57.807
a new Active Directory tab for the domain
controllers, and a new admin changes tab.

48
00:02:57.807 --> 00:03:03.125
Two are link-outs: Entra Health, because
its alerts aren't in Log Analytics,

49
00:03:03.125 --> 00:03:06.582
and the catalog tasks, which
live in the I T S M tool.

50
00:03:06.582 --> 00:03:08.483
Three of the ten are partial.

51
00:03:08.483 --> 00:03:11.828
The grants and admin
changes tabs list changes,

52
00:03:11.828 --> 00:03:15.432
but matching each to a ticket
stays in the I T S M tool.

53
00:03:15.432 --> 00:03:17.773
And the log review tab shows the queue,

54
00:03:17.773 --> 00:03:21.370
while the review itself
stays a recorded procedure.

55
00:03:25.120 --> 00:03:26.045
Who does what?

56
00:03:26.045 --> 00:03:31.545
Security operations owns the workspace,
the manifest and the freshness limits.

57
00:03:31.545 --> 00:03:35.195
Developers own the kit,
its queries and its tests.

58
00:03:35.195 --> 00:03:38.920
And the operators read their
own family's tabs each morning.

59
00:03:38.920 --> 00:03:43.183
Lab 1 needs nothing but PowerShell:
it reads the kit's own files.

60
00:03:43.183 --> 00:03:46.890
Lab 2 needs Log Analytics
Reader on a test workspace;

61
00:03:46.890 --> 00:03:49.908
it only reads the table
list, then signs out.

62
00:03:49.908 --> 00:03:54.750
A reviewer checks that each link-out
has its click path and an allowed host.

63
00:03:58.500 --> 00:04:01.800
Lab 1, in the kit's
folder, with no tenant.

64
00:04:01.800 --> 00:04:07.488
Step 1 lists the tabs: twelve of them,
with the telemetry row left off the slide.

65
00:04:07.488 --> 00:04:11.027
Notice that entra health and
tasks read no table at all,

66
00:04:11.027 --> 00:04:14.724
and that grants, admin changes
and log review are partial.

67
00:04:14.724 --> 00:04:18.103
Step 5 counts the tabs
by fit: four strong,

68
00:04:18.103 --> 00:04:22.650
three good, three partial,
one weak, and one link-out.

69
00:04:26.400 --> 00:04:28.388
Step 2 opens a link-out tab.

70
00:04:28.388 --> 00:04:33.359
Entra Health has no table: the tab sends
the operator to the Entra admin center,

71
00:04:33.359 --> 00:04:36.000
under Monitoring and health, then Health.

72
00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:41.412
Its G C C availability is not yet
specified, so nothing is built on its data.

73
00:04:41.412 --> 00:04:43.637
Step 3 opens a partial tab.

74
00:04:43.637 --> 00:04:48.606
The grants tab reads the Entra audit
logs, but its action says it plainly:

75
00:04:48.606 --> 00:04:51.975
each row is matched to its
ticket in the I T S M tool,

76
00:04:51.975 --> 00:04:55.190
and a grant with no
ticket opens a finding.

77
00:04:58.940 --> 00:05:01.890
Step 4 opens the new Active Directory tab.

78
00:05:01.890 --> 00:05:07.447
Five queries: each domain controller's
heartbeat, the replication probe's result,

79
00:05:07.447 --> 00:05:10.598
the high-rated security
events, the directory,

80
00:05:10.598 --> 00:05:14.828
replication and D N S errors,
and Defender for Identity alerts.

81
00:05:14.828 --> 00:05:19.466
Its link-outs are Connect Health for A
D D S and the Defender portal's alerts.

82
00:05:19.466 --> 00:05:24.132
Its G C C note says the workspace tables
are available in commercial Azure,

83
00:05:24.132 --> 00:05:28.799
Defender for Identity alerts are
generally available for G C C with their

84
00:05:28.799 --> 00:05:33.390
own license, and Connect Health is not
yet specified, so it stays a link-out.

85
00:05:33.390 --> 00:05:36.704
And the domain controllers
are never named in a query:

86
00:05:36.704 --> 00:05:39.560
they come from the settings, one row each.

87
00:05:43.310 --> 00:05:46.198
Step 6 reads the emergency access query.

88
00:05:46.198 --> 00:05:50.979
It's Microsoft's own sign-in query from
its emergency access guidance, with the

89
00:05:50.979 --> 00:05:55.760
accounts' object I D's taken from a
parameter instead of written into the query.

90
00:05:55.760 --> 00:05:59.673
And the comment says what matters:
the alert rule is the control.

91
00:05:59.673 --> 00:06:02.620
The tab only shows what the query found.

92
00:06:06.370 --> 00:06:10.532
Step 7 lists the thirteen
expected tables and their limits.

93
00:06:10.532 --> 00:06:13.633
Sign-in and audit logs: sixty minutes.

94
00:06:13.633 --> 00:06:16.491
The servers' heartbeats
and security events:

95
00:06:16.491 --> 00:06:20.432
sixty minutes too, because their
agents report continuously.

96
00:06:20.432 --> 00:06:24.874
Intune's compliance export: two thousand
eight hundred and eighty minutes,

97
00:06:24.874 --> 00:06:28.671
because it arrives once a day and
can take up to forty-eight hours.

98
00:06:28.671 --> 00:06:33.480
Step 8 runs the chapter's
tests: ten passed, none failed.

99
00:06:37.230 --> 00:06:42.555
Lab 2 runs in a test workspace, never
production, and the authors haven't run it.

100
00:06:42.555 --> 00:06:46.081
Sign in to commercial Azure,
list the workspace's tables,

101
00:06:46.081 --> 00:06:50.143
and print every table a tab reads
that the workspace doesn't have.

102
00:06:50.143 --> 00:06:52.043
It reads and changes nothing.

103
00:06:52.043 --> 00:06:56.285
Every name it prints is a source the
guide's first step still has to connect:

104
00:06:56.285 --> 00:06:59.206
the two probes' custom tables
until they're created,

105
00:06:59.206 --> 00:07:03.726
the Sentinel health table until health
monitoring is on, and the servers' event,

106
00:07:03.726 --> 00:07:06.438
heartbeat and security
event tables until the

107
00:07:06.438 --> 00:07:09.081
servers send through
the Azure Monitor Agent.

108
00:07:09.081 --> 00:07:10.500
Then sign out.

109
00:07:14.250 --> 00:07:16.875
Two outputs support federal requirements.

110
00:07:16.875 --> 00:07:21.102
Lab 1 shows every daily check has
a home, a table or a link-out,

111
00:07:21.102 --> 00:07:24.625
which supports continuous
monitoring, C A seven.

112
00:07:24.625 --> 00:07:28.235
Lab 2 shows the workspace
has each table a tab reads,

113
00:07:28.235 --> 00:07:31.588
which also supports system
monitoring, S I four.

114
00:07:31.588 --> 00:07:34.664
The manifest itself is
design, not evidence;

115
00:07:34.664 --> 00:07:37.399
the kit's records in later chapters are.

116
00:07:37.399 --> 00:07:40.784
The dangers: a tab built on
data G C C doesn't have,

117
00:07:40.784 --> 00:07:46.037
one freshness limit for every source,
and a partial tab read as complete.

118
00:07:46.037 --> 00:07:50.220
Next, Chapter 2 builds the workbook's
template and its validator.
